1
00:00:09,600 --> 00:00:18,070
The Cauchy–Schwarz inequality says that the inner product of two vectors is never larger than the product of their lengths.

2
00:00:18,070 --> 00:00:24,670
The difference between the two sides is a gap. It measures how far apart the two vectors point.

3
00:00:24,670 --> 00:00:31,830
When the vectors turn towards each other the gap shrinks, and it is zero exactly when they are parallel.

4
00:00:31,830 --> 00:00:38,967
This video is about what happens to that gap when every entry of the vectors is raised to a power.

5
00:00:39,367 --> 00:00:44,067
Raise every entry to a power p. Take the vectors (2, 1) and (1, 2).

6
00:00:44,067 --> 00:00:51,267
Big entries grow faster than small ones, so as p increases the two vectors turn apart, towards the axes.

7
00:00:51,267 --> 00:00:59,897
Johnston, Plosker, Torrance and Varona asked whether the gap of the powered vectors is at most the gap built from p-th powers.

8
00:00:59,897 --> 00:01:07,597
At p = 1 and at p = 2 the two sides are equal. Between 1 and 2 the left side is bigger, so the inequality fails.

9
00:01:07,597 --> 00:01:17,033
Beyond 2 the left side stays smaller. The question is whether this is true for every pair of vectors and every real p from 2 upwards.

10
00:01:17,433 --> 00:01:25,853
The case p = 2 began as a question on MathOverflow in 2018, and it was proved a year later in work on quantum entanglement.

11
00:01:25,853 --> 00:01:34,263
In 2025 Johnston, Plosker, Torrance and Varona proved it for every whole number p and showed that it fails between 1 and 2.

12
00:01:34,263 --> 00:01:42,513
They tested real exponents on more than ten billion random pairs and conjectured that every real p from 2 upwards works.

13
00:01:42,513 --> 00:01:47,313
This is Conjecture 5.1 of their paper, and it is now a theorem.

14
00:01:48,767 --> 00:01:55,417
For p = 2, write the products of all pairs of entries in a grid. This is the tensor square of v.

15
00:01:55,417 --> 00:01:58,997
Its diagonal is exactly the vector of squares, v².

16
00:01:58,997 --> 00:02:06,447
The full grids have inner product ⟨v, w⟩², so the right side of the inequality is the gap of the full grids.

17
00:02:06,447 --> 00:02:14,167
Keeping only the diagonal entries can only shrink the gap. The same works with p factors for any whole number p.

18
00:02:14,167 --> 00:02:20,437
For p = 2.5 there is no grid with two and a half factors. A different idea is needed.

19
00:02:21,900 --> 00:02:27,950
For each coordinate i, put the numbers v_i², v_i w_i and w_i² into a two by two matrix.

20
00:02:27,950 --> 00:02:36,730
For v = (2, 1) and w = (1, 2) there are two of them. Adding them gives a matrix of the lengths and the inner product of v and w.

21
00:02:36,730 --> 00:02:48,880
Raise every entry to the power p. If you power first and then add, you get the left side's matrix. If you add first and then power, you get the right side's matrix.

22
00:02:48,880 --> 00:02:54,970
The key fact: for p at least 2, adding and then powering always gives the bigger matrix.

23
00:02:54,970 --> 00:03:05,480
Bigger means that the difference is positive semidefinite: its diagonal is nonnegative and its corner entry is at most the geometric mean of the diagonal.

24
00:03:05,480 --> 00:03:12,620
This is a case of a 2015 theorem of Guillot, Khare and Rajaratnam, and its threshold is exactly 2.

25
00:03:16,100 --> 00:03:26,030
The positive semidefinite two by two matrices form a round cone. Up is the average of the diagonal, and the two sideways directions are the rest.

26
00:03:26,030 --> 00:03:34,900
The dot is the difference between the two matrices for v = (2, 1) and w = (1, 2). For p between 1 and 2 it lies outside the cone.

27
00:03:34,900 --> 00:03:41,170
At p = 2 it touches the boundary, and for every larger p it stays inside.

28
00:03:41,170 --> 00:03:50,299
The proof shows that for p at least 2 this happens for every pair of vectors, using a double integral formula for the powers.

29
00:03:51,699 --> 00:03:59,149
One last ingredient turns a matrix into a number: the square root of the diagonal product, minus the corner.

30
00:03:59,149 --> 00:04:04,599
Applied to the two matrices, it gives exactly the two sides of the inequality.

31
00:04:04,599 --> 00:04:13,299
And it only grows when its input moves up the cone. So the bigger matrix has the bigger gap, and the inequality is proved.

32
00:04:14,732 --> 00:04:23,312
The paper also finds every case of equality. For p above 2 only the obvious ones remain. For p = 2 there is one extra family.

33
00:04:23,312 --> 00:04:33,982
Weights can be put on the coordinates. The weighted inequality holds for every p ≥ 2 exactly when every product of two different weights is at least 1.

34
00:04:35,399 --> 00:04:44,679
Every step is also checked in Lean 4, a proof assistant, with the Mathlib library. This is the conjecture exactly as it is stated there.

35
00:04:44,679 --> 00:04:53,879
The check script confirms there are no gaps and no extra assumptions. The equality cases and the weighted result are checked too.

36
00:04:55,299 --> 00:05:02,329
Take two probability distributions, for example a physical system with two different energy functions.

37
00:05:02,329 --> 00:05:12,999
Raising every probability to a power p and renormalising is the same as cooling the system by a factor p. The distributions sharpen and their overlap falls.

38
00:05:12,999 --> 00:05:22,429
Applied to the square roots of the probabilities, the theorem limits how much the overlap can fall, for every cooling factor of 2 or more.

39
00:05:22,429 --> 00:05:32,399
Semi-supervised learning sharpens predictions in the same way. UDA uses temperature 0.4, which is p = 2.5, a case that needs the new theorem.

40
00:05:32,799 --> 00:05:41,119
So the conjecture is true, the threshold 2 comes from two by two matrices, and the whole argument is checked by computer.

41
00:05:41,119 --> 00:05:48,519
Thank you for watching. The paper, the Lean code and an applications report are linked below.
